````markdown
**Onomast: Lares (Tasked Spirit)**

Method: both repos cloned and read at source (`git clone` of `inkandswitch/onomancy` @ `1b0902c`, `inkandswitch/subduction` @ `61d1030`, plus `automerge/automerge-repo` branch metadata and the npm registry). Every `design/` and `specs/` document in both repos was read in full. URLs are to the file that carries the claim.

---

# A. `onomancy`

## A1. The data model — what a name binding IS

- There are **two different artifacts**, and conflating them is the first mistake. A *petname edge* is a bare key→value entry in an Automerge document's own top-level map. A *DNS binding* is a signed certificate + attached evidence. They are deliberately not the same shape. [design/anchors.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/anchors.md)
- **Petname edge**: the namestore is "a document's **own top-level map**, not a container within it" — flat, string-keyed, multi-segment keys like `"foo/bar/baz"`, values are **bare Automerge URLs**. No wrapping record, no metadata, no `met_as` field. [specs/path-resolution.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/path-resolution.md), [specs/anchoring/petname-anchor.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/anchoring/petname-anchor.md)
- The value **MUST be an Automerge scalar string, never a `Text` object** — because two writers splicing one stored identifier merge into "a third identifier *neither wrote* — a redirect no signature covers." The spec calls out that JS `doc[label] = "automerge:…"` silently produces a `Text`, so a namestore written the obvious way "resolves nowhere while looking correct from inside the application that wrote it." (`RawString` is the scalar spelling.) [specs/path-resolution.md §References](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/path-resolution.md)
- **Certificate** (`ONC\x00`), signed region, exact wire order: `root_doc` (32B ed25519 vk = document ID) · `signer` (32B vk) · `issued_at` (bijou64 seconds) · `hostname` (full DNS name, A-labels, lowercase, no trailing dot) · `heads` (sorted, dedup, count 0 = live) · `predecessor` (an embedded `ONS\x00` successor statement, len 0 = absent) · `signature` (64B ed25519 over fields 0–9). [specs/serialization.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/serialization.md), [design/certificate.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/certificate.md)
- **Attached (unsigned) region**, fields 11–16: `delegation_chain` (verbatim Keyhive `Signed<Delegation>` bytes), `lineage` (`ONR\x00` rotation statements), `chain` (RFC 4034 canonical DNSSEC wire links, root→leaf). This is the single best idea in the repo: "signed = what the signer asserts; attached = what the world can check." Refreshing a lapsed DNSSEC chain, or repairing a chain after generation rotation, needs **no key at all** — anyone can re-attach. Signing them would cause "freshness coupling," break "rotation repair," and freeze evidence. [design/certificate.md §Why the Attachments Are Unsigned](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/certificate.md)
- Deliberate exception: `predecessor` sits **inside** the signature "precisely because it is a claim an attacker must not strip or graft." [design/certificate.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/certificate.md)
- **Stored where**: in the document it binds, at the reserved top-level key `.well-known/onomancy/certificates` — verified in code as `CERTIFICATES_KEY`. Because the value is a *list* (not a reference), path resolution skips it: protocol data and names share one map with no registry. A publisher MAY instead store a one-hop reference to a separate document, and "usually should," so naming authority sits above collaboration authority. [onomancy_automerge/src/certificates.rs](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/onomancy_automerge/src/certificates.rs), [design/certificate.md §Retrieval](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/certificate.md)
- **`AuthorityVerifier` is exactly two functions.** From source:
  - `authorizes(root, signer, carriage) -> bool` — "a valid delegation chain rooting at `root`, terminating at `signer`, with the **delegating hop** held at admin access."
  - `on_path(carriage, generation) -> bool` — "whether `generation` lies on the delegation path in `carriage`, **at any depth**."
  [onomancy_protocol/src/verifier/state/authority_verifier.rs](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/onomancy_protocol/src/verifier/state/authority_verifier.rs)
- The real implementation, `KeyhiveAuthority`, does **not** reimplement Keyhive semantics — it **replays** the carriage's events into a throwaway `keyhive_core` 0.5 instance, retrying to a fixpoint so entry order need only be "dependency-*compatible*, not perfectly sorted," then asks the materialized graph membership questions. Stateless between calls; every failure degrades to refusal. Two rules beyond the graph: the doc-root key speaks for its own document with an empty chain, and the bar is on the **delegating** hop (the signer's own access rank is irrelevant — which is why a successor generation key can sign a rotation statement while holding no document authority). Known gap in code: `sanctioned` is **direct membership only**; nested-group delegation chains are future work with an `#[ignore]`d test as the executable marker. [onomancy_keyhive/src/authority.rs](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/onomancy_keyhive/src/authority.rs), [onomancer/README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/onomancer/README.md)
- **Verification order** for a certificate: decode → signature under `signer` → delegation chain valid (root→signer, admin delegating hop) → DNSSEC chain from baked-in KSK → TXT `p=` == `cert.root_doc` → TXT owner == `cert.hostname` → serial ratchet / generation key / succession. Output is a **graded** `Verified { verified_at, chain_window }`, not a boolean. [design/certificate.md §Verification](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/certificate.md)
- Type-state: raw bytes give **no access** to claims; `Signed<T> → try_verify → Verified<T>` is the only path. "Forgetting to verify is a type error, not a bug class." [design/certificate.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/certificate.md)
- The whole verifier state is a **pure derivation**: `derive(store, now, decisions)`, 8 normative stages, acyclic and total. "Sync is set union… arrival order is never an input… Gossip races decide nothing." **No derived state ever crosses a boundary** — no ratchet counters or "I saw X" marks on the wire. [specs/anchoring/binding-cache.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/anchoring/binding-cache.md)

## A2. The three name forms, their roots, and composition

| Form | Root of trust | Resolution |
|---|---|---|
| `automerge:2nBe…/foo` | the URL **is** the doc ID (32-byte ed25519 vk, bs58check) — self-certifying | no anchoring step at all |
| `~/bob/pics` | **you** — your own signed root document | root store is local, always present, zero verification |
| `@expede.wtf/foo` | baked-in IANA root KSK → DS/DNSKEY/RRSIG → TXT → certificate | binding cache or fetch, then chain validation |

[specs/README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/README.md), [design/names.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/names.md)

- **The anchor is a parse-time fact.** No fallback, no precedence. A single spelling with a "local wins / DNS wins" rule was considered and rejected: "a name whose meaning depends on lookup order is an attack surface," and precedence makes meaning shift with connectivity — "a TOCTOU race on the trust anchor itself." Anchor disjointness is Lean theorem #1, named "the anti-phishing theorem." [design/names.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/names.md), [design/verification.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/verification.md)
- Dotless `@bob` is a **flat parse error**, aligned with ICANN SAC053 — "the `@bob` vs `@bob.co` near-miss phishing class is deleted, not mitigated." Legacy 16-byte Automerge doc IDs get a distinct rejection: valid URLs, but not self-certifying. [design/names.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/names.md)
- **Composition — the answer to "can a petname point at a DNSSEC name" is NO, and this is load-bearing.** Edges hold document references, never names: "A reference MUST NOT contain a name (of any anchor family) that would be re-parsed and re-resolved." Error E5 / P6. This is not hygiene — it is the **termination proof**: each hop consumes ≥1 segment, so resolution terminates in at most `len(segments)` hops, and "Resolvers MUST NOT impose a hop limit as a substitute for the no-symlink invariant." Their Lean note is elegant: if an edge type ever gains a name-valued variant, `resolve` stops being structurally recursive and the model **fails to compile**. [specs/path-resolution.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/path-resolution.md), [design/verification.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/verification.md)
- What *does* compose: all three converge on the same document ID, so `~/bob` and `@bob.example` "simply converge on the same document ID." An offline QR introduction roots as a petname on day 0; a certificate arriving on day 30 "adds a spelling; it MUST NOT migrate identity, rewrite the petname edge, or re-key anything." [specs/anchoring/petname-anchor.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/anchoring/petname-anchor.md)
- Walk semantics: **greedy longest-key match, no backtracking**, at segment boundaries. Longest-match "keeps authority with the namestore closest to the name's owner." No backtracking because it "would make resolution outcomes depend on the availability of downstream namestores." [specs/path-resolution.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/path-resolution.md)
- Open tension they flag themselves: vanilla Automerge tooling descending `automerge:‹id›/foo/bar` will **not** find the flat key `"foo/bar"`. "Either vanilla compatibility is a non-goal, or the namestore layout needs to reconcile with upstream path semantics." [design/names.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/names.md)

## A3. The DNSSEC path

- One required record: `_onomancy.<name>. IN TXT "v=ONO0;k=ed25519;n=<serial>;g=<b64 gen key>;p=<b64 doc ID>"`. Fixed field order, single `;` separator, no whitespace, ≤133 octets; decoders MUST reject unknown/reordered fields, leading zeros, malformed base64 — "the record is a trust root, and parser leniency is attack surface." Extension is by tag bump (`ONO1`), never field tolerance. [design/dns-binding.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/dns-binding.md), [specs/serialization.md §TXT Record Grammar](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/serialization.md)
- An optional SVCB record is a **transport hint, not a canonical location** — their analogy: "the TXT `p=` is the infohash (all of the authority), the hints are trackers (none of it), and gossip is PEX." [design/dns-binding.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/dns-binding.md)
- **Neither direction alone is a binding.** "Anyone controlling any signed zone can name any document" — the zone says which document; the certificate says which hostnames the document accepts; both must agree. [README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/README.md)
- **Is it offline-verifiable? Yes — this is the whole point.** Chains validate locally from the baked-in anchor; verifiers MUST "never delegate to a resolver's AD bit." Verified bindings are self-authenticating records that gossip over Bluetooth/QR and re-verify from the receiver's own KSK. "After the first fetch, resolution never queries the network again — this is the record-first property." [GUIDANCE.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/GUIDANCE.md), [design/README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/README.md)
- Freshness is **graded, not boolean**: `fresh ✓ / stale ⚠ / invalid ✗`, where stale means "once-valid; window has lapsed." "Staleness is a risk signal, not a forgery signal." Hard-rejecting expired chains "would break offline gossip within weeks." [design/dns-binding.md §Graded Freshness](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/dns-binding.md)
- Requirements on DNS operators: DNSSEC-signed zone (algorithms 8/13/15); low TTL (~5–15 min); serials `max(now_ms, last+1)`; **wildcard-synthesized answers are REJECTED** (their proof would be a negative proof); denial-of-existence is outside the protocol at v0 — "a missing record is always *absence not proven*," failing toward retention; unknown signature algorithms yield ✗, not the resolver-world "insecure" downgrade. [GUIDANCE.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/GUIDANCE.md), [design/dns-binding.md §Chain Validation](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/dns-binding.md)
- **KSK rollover — docs and code disagree, and the code is ahead.** `design/dns-binding.md` says "At v0 a single 2024 IANA KSK is baked in" and calls a trust-anchor *set* "future work." The source already ships an anchor **set** in DS form: `iana_root_anchors()` returns both **KSK-2017 (key tag 20326)** and **KSK-2024 (key tag 38696)**, RSA_SHA256, SHA-256 DS digests, with the comment "the validator takes an anchor **set**, never a single anchor: RFC 5011-style KSK rollovers overlap for months, and gossiped chains must verify on either side of the boundary." Trust that file, not the design doc. [onomancy_dnssec/src/trust_anchor.rs](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/onomancy_dnssec/src/trust_anchor.rs) vs [design/dns-binding.md §KSK Rollover (Open)](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/dns-binding.md)
- **What breaks at rollover**: nothing, for an online client shipped during the overlap. What breaks is "offline anchor rot" — a client offline across a *compromise-and-revocation* accepts freshly-forged chains, "because freshness measures signature windows, not key legitimacy," and there is no mitigation for fully-offline clients. Their calibration: one rollover (2018) and one ceremonial revocation (2019) ever, so app-update cadence outruns it. [design/security.md §Offline Anchor Rot](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/security.md)

## A4. Revocation and rotation

- **`p=` never changes for key rotation.** Rotation happens in the delegation graph; changing `p=` means **document migration** (a genuinely new identity). [design/dns-binding.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/dns-binding.md)
- **The generation key is the cleverest mechanism in the repo.** `g=` names one **chokepoint** every certificate's delegation chain must pass through, at any depth. Grants under the current generation cost no DNS touch. Revocation = revoke `admin→Gₙ`, mint `Gₙ₊₁`, re-delegate survivors, publish new `g=`. "The verifier's check is positive path membership, no list, no sync." Rationale: "negative facts can't travel in attacker-supplied records." It is "the DS/KSK pattern one level up," and the decentralized cousin of ATProto's rotation-key hierarchy — zone-attested rather than directory-attested, no sequencer, no 72-hour history rewrite. [design/dns-binding.md §Generation Key](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/dns-binding.md), [design/comparisons/README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/comparisons/README.md)
- **Is there expiry? Explicitly no.** "Expiry is at odds with local-first"; "No mechanism… no death clock on a binding." Freshness is graded at the chain layer instead. [design/README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/README.md), [design/comparisons/GNS.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/comparisons/GNS.md)
- **Is there a tombstone? Also no, and they know it hurts.** There are "no absence items: negative proofs are outside the protocol at v0 — unbinding awaits a future owner-signed unbind statement." So you cannot currently prove a name was *un*bound. [specs/anchoring/binding-cache.md §The Store](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/anchoring/binding-cache.md)
- Replay ratchet: monotone serial `n`; stale records must exceed; **fresh chains may move it in either direction** (surfaced as a ratchet-reset event); serials >5 min in the future are **deferred**, not rejected — "they ripen as the clock advances, so skew failures are transient delays." A transient zone attacker can poison at most ~5 min ahead. Accepted residual: a fully offline verifier that ate poison heals only via a manual per-name "reset trust." [design/dns-binding.md §Serial Ratchet](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/dns-binding.md), [design/security.md §Ratchet Poisoning](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/security.md)
- Two statement types scope **oppositely, on purpose**: rotation statements (`ONR\x00`) bind `(root_doc, Gₙ, Gₙ₊₁)` with **no hostname** — "a revoked generation must die across every name bound to the document, in one ceremony"; successor statements (`ONS\x00`) bind `(hostname, predecessor_doc, successor_doc)` **with** the hostname inside the signature, "because migration is per-name and its proof must not be replayable across names." [specs/serialization.md §Statement Encodings](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/serialization.md)
- **Migration continuity**: a `p=` change with a valid `predecessor` looks routine; without one it is **surfaced, never silent**, and graded by **tenure** — the displaced binding's accumulated window evidence in the verifier's own store, "history a capture-era newcomer can neither fake nor remove." Proofs are **relative evidence**: on first contact they confer nothing ("two attacker documents vouching for each other is not continuity"). [design/certificate.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/certificate.md), [design/security.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/security.md)
- **The mutual backstop** (worth stealing wholesale): lose the keys → the *name* rescues you (migrate `p=`, contacts re-pin); lose the name → the *keys* rescue you (identity, data, delegations, pinned edges all survive). "Neither anchor alone is resilient; the pair is." [design/anchors.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/anchors.md)
- Also ruled: **transparency is advisory, forever.** CT-style enforcement is "rejected outright: it breaks record-first offline verification, makes log operators a liveness and governance dependency, and creates a globally enumerable roster of participants." [design/security.md §Transparency Is Advisory, Forever](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/security.md)

## A5. Petname semantics and the Zooko lineage

- **Yes, explicitly Zooko-aware, and it refuses to squat one corner.** There is a literal Zooko's-triangle table (Doc: secure+global; DNS: secure*+global+memorable; Petname: secure+memorable) with the footnote "* modulo trust in DNSSEC and the IANA root KSK." Conclusion: "Rather than squatting one corner, Onomancy lets each name occupy the corner that fits, with documents as the common substrate." And in limitations: "Zooko's triangle is navigated, not defeated." [design/anchors.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/anchors.md), [design/limitations.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/limitations.md)
- **Yes, it cites Stiegler directly.** The petname spec links `http://www.skyhunter.com/marcs/petnames/IntroPetNames.html` (Marc Stiegler, *An Introduction to Petname Systems*) and states: "In petname-system terms, **Stiegler's classic trio** survives the layout — relocated, not reduced: the value is the *key*, the map key is the *petname* (trusted, local, yours), and the *alleged name* is an untrusted claim stored alongside the other binding claims in the cache. The model's anti-spoofing property rests on never letting an alleged name occupy a petname slot without deliberate human action." [specs/anchoring/petname-anchor.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/anchoring/petname-anchor.md)
- SDSI/SPKI is named as "the common ancestor — local names over keys." **No source found** for a direct Mark Miller citation anywhere in the repo. [design/comparisons/README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/comparisons/README.md)
- The trust statement for `~` is stated in one sentence: "*You* (whoever controls the signing keys of this device's root document) previously bound this label to this document reference." `~` names **MUST NOT** appear in any wire format; serializers MUST reject them; a `~` arriving over the wire is malformed input. [specs/anchoring/petname-anchor.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/anchoring/petname-anchor.md)
- Divergence is `known_hosts` semantics done properly: detected by a **document-ID join** against the binding cache — never per-edge metadata — MUST NOT auto-repoint, SHOULD NOT hard-fail, MUST offer explicit re-pin. And prompts "MUST state the **direction and grade** of the evidence": a stale capture-era certificate "must not out-argue an in-person introduction by borrowing the word 'verified.'" [specs/anchoring/petname-anchor.md §Divergence and Re-Pin](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/anchoring/petname-anchor.md), [specs/anchoring/binding-cache.md §Prompt Grading](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/anchoring/binding-cache.md)
- **Honest about the floor**: "Onomancy makes impersonating an *established relationship* cryptographically hard, and does approximately nothing about deceiving someone who has no relationship yet." First contact is TOFU, "same epistemics as SSH, stated rather than hidden." [design/limitations.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/limitations.md)
- Closest living relative is **GNS (RFC 9498)**, with a full concept map and a stated one-line split: "GNS says *memorable-global names aren't worth a coercible root*; Onomancy says *they are, exactly once, if the root can be fired without taking your identity with it*." GNS bet on the network (DHT ⇒ expiry, query privacy, PoW revocation flood); Onomancy bet on the replica (local reads ⇒ offline resolution, `Partial(UnsyncedTarget)` instead of timeouts, revocation as a delegation edge). Notable admission: GNS's query privacy "has no analogue here. Our namestores are plaintext to anyone we sync with." [design/comparisons/GNS.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/comparisons/GNS.md)

## A6. Maturity

- **Alpha, and says so at the top of every README**: "Interfaces, wire formats, and specifications change without notice — use at your own risk." Crate versions all **0.2.0**; npm `@inkandswitch/onomancy` 0.2.0. Repo history is short (~21 commits on `main`) — this is young. [README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/README.md), [onomancy_wasm/package.json](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/onomancy_wasm/package.json)
- Scale: **~27.4k lines of Rust**, 10 workspace crates, **300 `#[test]` + 29 `#[wasm_bindgen_test]` + 30 bolero property checks**, plus DNSSEC golden vectors, replay/real-world tests, and a Playwright e2e suite over the Wasm bindings.
- Architecture is the strong part: strictly sans-IO. "**Time is data.** No machine reads a clock… The only `now()` call in the workspace lives in the `onomancer` binary." "**Transport is untrusted by design.** The stub resolver uses weak query IDs and no transport security on purpose." "**Framability is not validity.**" [HACKING.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/HACKING.md)
- **Is it usable?** The verifier stack runs end-to-end today — CLI, Node, and browser against live DNS, with `KeyhiveAuthority` doing real carriage verification and `bind`/`record` minting real generation carriages. The named v0 gaps, from their own caveats: document **content** authorship is not checkable (resolutions grade `carriage-verified` at best), `sanctioned` is direct-membership only, and **no verb writes the decision document**, so acceptance-on-use "never fires from this CLI" — meaning the entire incumbency/pending doctrine in the binding-cache spec is **specified but not exercised**. [onomancer/README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/onomancer/README.md)
- Formal verification: **aspirational, not delivered.** Lean 4 model with 7 numbered theorems — statuses are "modeled below" (1–3), "sketched" (4), "pending codec" (5), "restate… and fold into 7" (6), "pending" (7). The Lean directory does not exist yet ("Repository Layout (**when the toolchain lands**)"). [design/verification.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/verification.md)
- Dependency risk you must price: the certificate carries **verbatim Keyhive `Signed<Delegation>` bytes**, and the spec's own warning says "a Keyhive encoding change is a certificate format change (new format tag)." `keyhive_core = "0.5" # pre-alpha`. Their Ask 1 upstream is exactly this: "We don't need the format to be *pretty*; we need `decode(bytes)` to work in ten years." [specs/serialization.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/serialization.md), [design/keyhive-coordination.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/keyhive-coordination.md), [Cargo.toml](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/Cargo.toml)

## A7. Myth, ritual, naming-as-invocation

This one will disappoint you, and the disappointment is itself the finding.

- **The mythic framing is confined to the name and one word.** The only explicit gloss: "*Onomancy* is the protocol (grammar, records, certificate, and resolution semantics) and *Onomancer* is the **reference implementation (the tool that helps you practice onomancy)**." Reinforced in the README: "Libraries implement the protocol (`onomancy_*`); agents that practice it are onomancers (`onomancer_*`)." [specs/README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/README.md), [README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/README.md)
- The one sustained ritual metaphor is **"ceremony,"** and it is doing real technical work: `onomancy_publish` holds "Ceremonies (bind · refresh · rotate · migrate) emitting self-verified Plans"; the revocation ceremony is a MUST; "the cold key surfaces only for genuine ceremonies: new bindings, migration, revocation"; storage "grows with **ceremonies and introductions**… never with time, resolution count, or refresh churn." A ceremony is precisely *the moment a cold key comes out*. [onomancy_publish/README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/onomancy_publish/README.md), [design/certificate.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/certificate.md), [specs/anchoring/binding-cache.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/anchoring/binding-cache.md)
- **There is no "true name" doctrine, no invocation language, no divination framing anywhere.** I swept all `.md` for *myth · ritual · true name · magic · incantation · invocation · spell · sorcery · scry · augury · etymology* — zero hits except one, which is a **repudiation**: `limitations.md` opens "Naming systems attract **magical thinking** — 'cryptographically verified' reads as 'safe' — so this document states the boundaries plainly," and closes: "Every system in this space eventually publishes claims like 'phishing-proof naming.' When this one is tempted, the table above is the **pre-registered rebuttal**." [design/limitations.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/limitations.md)
- Read straight: they took a divinatory name and then wrote a document whose explicit job is to *stop the name from meaning anything*. The mythic surface is one word deep; the discipline underneath is anti-mythic on purpose.

---

# B. `subduction`

## B1. Sedimentree, properly

- **What it is**: "a data structure which recursively compresses ranges of a commit graph in such a way that older commits (those close to the root) are compressed in larger chunks than newer commits. We can imagine the older chunks as being 'underneath' newer chunks, like the strata of a sedimentary rock." The canonical design doc lives in the **Keyhive** repo and Subduction's README links to it as authoritative. [keyhive/design/sedimentree.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/keyhive/blob/main/design/sedimentree.md), [subduction/README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/README.md)
- **Vocabulary drift, note it**: the original doc says *stratum* / *level*; the Subduction implementation's type is **`Fragment`** at a **`Depth`**, while the prose still says "strata." Same thing. [design/sedimentree.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/sedimentree.md), [sedimentree_core/src/depth.rs](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/sedimentree_core/src/depth.rs)
- **Three ingredients**, all of which must produce identical results on peers with divergent graphs:
  1. **Reverse depth-first traversal** of the *reversed* change graph (heads → parents). Forward DFS is disastrous: a concurrent change reorders the traversal and "two peers with the same chunk boundaries disagree on the contents of the second chunk." Parents never change, so reversing gives a stable order.
  2. **Boundaries from the hash itself** — no coordination, no negotiation. A commit whose digest has ≥1 leading zero *byte* can head a depth-1 fragment; ≥2, depth 2; etc.
  3. **Checkpoint commits retained forever** — the level-1 boundaries interior to a fragment — which is what lets a peer decide whether one fragment *supports* (fully contains) another without holding the data. Start+end hashes alone are provably insufficient; the doc walks the counterexample.
  [keyhive/design/sedimentree.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/keyhive/blob/main/design/sedimentree.md)
- **Why leading zeros specifically**: it is a *self-balancing, content-derived, coordination-free* boundary rule. "Because hashes are distributed uniformly… the chance of having $n$ trailing zeros is $10^{-n}$, which means we will have a hash boundary approximately every $10^{n}$ changes"; the base is a free parameter. Subduction picks base 256 (leading zero **bytes**): depth 0 = 255/256 of commits, depth 1 ≈ 1/256, depth 2 ≈ 1/65536. It is a distributed, stateless analogue of content-defined chunking. Crucially it is **swappable** — `DepthMetric` is a trait; `CountLeadingZeroBytes` is merely the default. [keyhive/design/sedimentree.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/keyhive/blob/main/design/sedimentree.md), [design/sedimentree.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/sedimentree.md), [sedimentree_core/src/depth.rs](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/sedimentree_core/src/depth.rs)
- **Compaction/minimization**: sort fragments by depth → drop fragments fully supported by deeper ones → drop commits covered by fragment boundaries. "Two trees with the same content will have identical minimal representations," and `minimal_hash` (BLAKE3 over the sorted digest set of the minimized tree) gives "identical content → identical hash, **regardless of sync order**." [design/sedimentree.md §Minimization](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/sedimentree.md)
- **Guarantees claimed**: content-addressed, deterministic, opaque-friendly, self-balancing, **idempotent**, **commutative** ("merge order doesn't matter"). Checkpoints are 12-byte truncated digests (96-bit) — ~62% wire saving, ~10⁻¹⁷ collision at 1M items. [design/sedimentree.md §Properties](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/sedimentree.md)
- **The motivating problem** is worth internalizing: under E2EE the sync server has only the commit graph, so it can no longer produce the compressed whole-document blob that makes initial sync cheap; and federating ciphertext servers "will be forced to download all of the commit DAG because otherwise they have no way of knowing whether they have all the content." Sedimentree exists to give **predictable performance on both plaintext and ciphertext nodes**. [keyhive/design/sedimentree.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/keyhive/blob/main/design/sedimentree.md)

## B2. Syncing without decrypting — and the leakage surface

- The mechanism: sync operates purely on **metadata** — digests, parent digests, boundaries, checkpoints, blob sizes. "Sedimentree works with opaque, encrypted payloads"; "Content-addressed storage reveals nothing about plaintext." Content encryption is explicitly **out of scope — "Application responsibility."** Subduction itself encrypts nothing. [design/security/threats.md T8](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/security/threats.md), [design/security/README.md §Out of Scope](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/security/README.md)
- **What a peer learns, concretely, from the wire format** — this is bigger than the docs admit, and you should price it:
  - `SedimentreeId` (32B, the document identifier) on every message — full document-set visibility for anything it relays.
  - **`IssuerVK` — the author's raw Ed25519 verifying key, 32 bytes in the clear, on every `Signed<LooseCommit>` and `Signed<Fragment>`.** Authorization is checked against the *author*, not the sender, which is a good property; the cost is that the relay learns the complete **authorship graph** per document. [design/protocol.md §LooseCommit](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/protocol.md)
  - The full **causal DAG shape**: parent digests, sorted, 32B each, up to 255 parents.
  - **`BlobSize` in the clear** (bijou64), so per-edit sizes leak.
  - Subscription sets, timing, and sync frequency per peer.
- Their own residual: "Metadata analysis (size, timing) may reveal information. Consider padding for high-security applications." That is the entire mitigation offered. [design/security/threats.md T8](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/security/threats.md)
- Fingerprints are computed over **causal identity only** (commit: `digest()`; fragment: `head + boundary`), deliberately excluding `blob_meta`, so "two differently-compressed fragments covering the same causal range are considered equivalent." Nice forward-compat move, and it also means re-encryption/re-compression doesn't force a resync. [design/sync/batch.md §Causal Identity](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/sync/batch.md)

## B3. The wire protocol, the handshake, and the clock — read this section twice

- **Two codecs, not one.** The sedimentree/subduction path is the hand-rolled canonical binary codec: 4-byte schema header `[prefix0, prefix1, type_byte, version]`, prefixes `ST` (sedimentree) / `SU` (subduction); big-endian; **bijou64** bijective varints; **sorted, duplicate-free arrays** whose decoders reject unsorted input; **no optional fields or defaults**; "Decoders reject messages with unknown prefixes or unsupported versions." Signature covers everything before it: schema ‖ IssuerVK ‖ fields. Rejecting CBOR is explicit — "No CBOR/MessagePack edge cases or implementation variance." [design/protocol.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/protocol.md)
- **But `subduction_keyhive` uses CBOR (`ciborium`)** for its own `Message` enum — its round-trip tests are literally `ciborium::into_writer` / `from_reader`. So the "no CBOR" property holds for the sync/sedimentree layer only, not for the Keyhive sub-protocol. [subduction_keyhive/src/message.rs](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/subduction_keyhive/src/message.rs)
- **Scope binding**: `LooseCommit` and `Fragment` embed `SedimentreeId` *inside the signed bytes* — "preventing data from being uploaded to the wrong document." [design/protocol.md §Scope Binding](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/protocol.md)
- Message tags: `0x00` LooseCommit · `0x01` Fragment · `0x02`/`0x03` **retired** (was BlobsRequest/Response; blobs now travel inline, tag values left unused "so the remaining tags stay stable") · `0x04` BatchSyncRequest · `0x05` BatchSyncResponse · `0x06` RemoveSubscriptions · `0x07` DataRequestRejected · `0x08` HeadsUpdate. Envelope `SUM\x00` + 4B size + 1B tag. Sync messages are **not signed** — authenticated by the connection. Max message 50 MiB. [design/protocol.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/protocol.md)
- **Handshake, exact sequence** (symmetric — "Either peer can initiate… fully peer-to-peer"; server-ness is a deployment choice):
  1. Initiator → `Signed<Challenge> { audience, timestamp, nonce }` (157B inner, `SUH\x00`).
  2. Responder verifies signature → validates **audience** → validates **timestamp within `max_drift`** → claims `(PeerId, Nonce)` in the `NonceCache` **only after signature verification passes** ("Recording failed attempts would allow attackers to exhaust the cache").
  3. Responder → `Signed<Response> { challenge_digest = BLAKE3(challenge), server_timestamp }` (140B, `SUR\x00`).
  4. Initiator verifies signature and digest binding.
  Rejections are **unsigned** (9B) and their `server_timestamp` is informational only.
  [design/handshake.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/handshake.md), [design/protocol.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/protocol.md)
- **Audience binding** has two modes: `Known(PeerId)` and `Discover(BLAKE3(service_name))`. It "prevents misdirection to the wrong peer" — i.e. a challenge captured from one endpoint can't be replayed at another. [design/handshake.md §Audience Modes](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/handshake.md)
- **What the 60s freshness window protects against**: it bounds the replay window for a captured `Signed<Challenge>`. The nonce cache is the actual anti-replay mechanism; the timestamp exists to keep that cache **bounded** — 4 buckets × 3 min = 12-minute window, sized to cover `MAX_PLAUSIBLE_DRIFT` (±10 min) plus 2 min buffer, with lazy GC on bucket rotation and no background task. Memory: 48 B/entry, ~3.5 MB at 100 handshakes/sec. Without a timestamp you would need an unbounded nonce set. Residual, stated: "Within the timestamp window, an attacker with a privileged network position could attempt replay before nonce is recorded. Mitigated by TLS." [design/handshake.md §Nonce Tracking](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/handshake.md), [subduction_core/src/nonce_cache.rs](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/subduction_core/src/nonce_cache.rs), [design/security/threats.md T2](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/security/threats.md)
- **The clockless answer — the sharp part for us.** Four separate facts, and they don't all point the same way:
  1. **Core is sans-IO about time.** `now` is a *parameter* to `handshake::respond`/`verify_challenge`, and `max_drift` is a *parameter* (`Duration::from_secs(60)` in the documented call, "default: 60 seconds… configurable"). `TimestampSeconds::now()` appears **only in transports and the CLI** (`subduction_websocket`, `subduction_http_longpoll`, `subduction_iroh`, `subduction_cli`), never in `subduction_core`'s protocol logic. [subduction_core/src/handshake.rs](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/subduction_core/src/handshake.rs), [design/handshake.md §High-Level API](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/handshake.md)
  2. **But an absolute wall clock is mandatory to connect at all.** `Challenge.timestamp` is Unix seconds. A peer with no wall clock cannot mint an acceptable challenge; a peer whose clock is wrong by >`max_drift` gets `Rejection::ClockDrift`. There is no zero-knowledge-of-time path.
  3. **The escape hatch is real but constrained.** `max_drift` is caller-supplied so it *can* be widened, and `NonceCache::new(bucket_duration)` is constructible with any bucket duration — the 3-minute default is `DEFAULT_BUCKET_DURATION`, not a constant of the protocol. So a deployment *can* trade replay-window for clock-freedom, at linear memory cost. But `MAX_PLAUSIBLE_DRIFT = 10 min` is a hard `const` capping **drift correction**, and the docs' 12-minute window is sized against it — widening `max_drift` past the nonce window silently reopens replay. [subduction_core/src/handshake.rs](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/subduction_core/src/handshake.rs), [subduction_core/src/nonce_cache.rs](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/subduction_core/src/nonce_cache.rs)
  4. **After the handshake, sync is genuinely clock-free.** Ordering uses a **per-peer monotonic counter** (`PeerCounter` / `RemoteHeads.counter`), not timestamps — "Receivers use this to detect and discard out-of-order or stale heads on non-TCP transports." That is a logical clock, and it is the right one. The *ephemeral* layer, by contrast, does a "message-age check" against a wall clock (`JsClock` = `Date.now()`). [design/sync/batch.md §RemoteHeads](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/sync/batch.md), [subduction_core/src/peer/counter.rs](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/subduction_core/src/peer/counter.rs), [subduction_wasm/src/clock.rs](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/subduction_wasm/src/clock.rs)
  - Their own assumption doc is candid: "**Monotonic Time for Nonce Cache** — System time does not jump backwards significantly… Large backward jumps could prematurely expire valid entries **or allow replay of 'expired' nonces**." Consequence of violation: "Replay attack window." [design/assumptions.md §Time](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/assumptions.md)
- One more property we care about: **authorization is checked against the author (signature issuer), never the sender.** "This enables multi-hop forwarding while maintaining authorship accountability." Relays are byte couriers. [design/security/rationale.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/security/rationale.md)

## B4. Keyhive integration

- **Clean separation, stated as a principle**: "Authentication (handshake) is separate from authorization (policy)." Handshake answers *who is connecting*; `ConnectionPolicy` answers *can they*. The handshake doc's own footnote: "This pattern is used only for *authentication*… For *authorization*, see your policy layer (e.g., Keyhive)." [design/README.md §Design Principles](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/README.md), [design/handshake.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/handshake.md)
- The seam is two traits in `subduction_core::policy`: `ConnectionPolicy` and `StoragePolicy` (`authorize_fetch` / `authorize_put` / **`filter_authorized_fetch`** — the batch variant, so "a Keyhive-based policy can look up the document's membership once and check if the peer has `Pull` access"). `SubductionKeyhive` in `subduction_keyhive/src/policy.rs` implements both. **Revocation needs no protocol message**: "the server simply stops forwarding — the peer fails the `filter_authorized_fetch` check. No explicit 'revocation notification' is needed from Subduction." [subduction_keyhive/src/policy.rs](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/subduction_keyhive/src/policy.rs), [design/sync/subscriptions.md §Revocation](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/sync/subscriptions.md)
- **README staleness to know about**: the README's architecture graph and crate table list `subduction_keyhive_policy` as a separate crate. **It does not exist** — no such directory, not in `[workspace] members`. The policy lives inside `subduction_keyhive` behind the `handler` feature. [README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/README.md) vs [Cargo.toml](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/Cargo.toml)
- **`@automerge/automerge-subduction`** is the npm package built from the `automerge_subduction_wasm` crate: "combines the Automerge document ID helpers (e.g. `commitIdOfBase58Id`) with `subduction_wasm` to provide a complete solution for syncing Automerge documents in browser and Node.js." Crate version 0.17.0; npm `latest` is **0.16.1**. It ships a `/slim` entry (type-only / no auto-Wasm-init) alongside the full entry. Also carries its own "early release preview… DO NOT use for production" banner. [automerge_subduction_wasm/README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/automerge_subduction_wasm/README.md), [automerge_subduction_wasm/package.json](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/automerge_subduction_wasm/package.json), npm registry `@automerge/automerge-subduction` dist-tags → `{"latest":"0.16.1"}`
- **#258 "Use op-set digests for keyhive sync checks"** (commit `03d322c`, John Mumm, 2026-06-27) — I read the diff. Before: a lightweight `SyncCheck` compared only *counts* (`sender_total`, `sender_syncpoint` cross-matched against the local syncpoint). Two peers with equal op **counts** but different op **sets** would falsely conclude "in sync." The fix adds `sender_digest: [u8; 32]` to `Message::SyncCheck` — an **order-independent XOR fold of the per-pair op-hash set** (`pair_set_digest`) — and makes `in_sync` require `digests_match` as well. Empty set → all zeros, which "matches a peer that has not yet sent a digest," so it degrades gracefully against older peers. On mismatch it "falls back to full sync." Tests assert the digest is order-independent, that an empty set is zero, that different sets differ, and — the load-bearing one — `resolve_sync_check_falls_back_on_digest_mismatch_despite_matching_counts`. It is a cheap set-equality probe, not a set-reconciliation structure. [commit 03d322c](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/commit/03d322c0313578e232d989697d0f47f772083f25), [subduction_keyhive/src/protocol.rs](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/subduction_keyhive/src/protocol.rs), [subduction_keyhive/src/syncpoints.rs](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/subduction_keyhive/src/syncpoints.rs)
  - Caveat for us: **XOR folding is not collision-resistant against an adversary who chooses op hashes** (any pair of identical elements cancels; an attacker with control over op hashes can forge a matching digest). It is fine as a probe whose only failure mode is a spurious full sync — but do not promote it to a proof of set equality.

## B5. Storage

- The contract is the `Storage` trait in `subduction_core` (`save_fragment` / `save_commit` / `save_blob` / `load_*` / `save_batch`), with three assumptions the protocol will not enforce for you: **idempotency** ("storing the same data twice has the same effect as storing it once"), **durability** ("once a write returns success, the data will survive restarts" — the protocol has **no journaling or WAL**), and **per-commit atomicity** ("a commit and its associated blob must be stored together or not at all"). **Multi-commit atomicity is explicitly not assumed.** Also: "**Storage Is Trusted** — a malicious storage layer could violate sync correctness." [design/assumptions.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/assumptions.md)
- Backends in-tree: `MemoryStorage`, `sedimentree_fs_storage` (content-addressed files, atomic `.tmp`+rename, **CBOR via minicbor** for commit/fragment records), `subduction_redb_storage` (the native server default), and IndexedDB in `sedimentree_wasm`. [sedimentree_fs_storage/README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/sedimentree_fs_storage/README.md), [sedimentree_wasm/src/storage/idb.rs](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/sedimentree_wasm/src/storage/idb.rs)
- **redb is a hybrid, and the reasoning is measured, not assumed**: metadata + blobs ≤ **16 KiB** (`DEFAULT_INLINE_THRESHOLD`) go in a transactional B+tree; larger blobs are flat content-addressed files in 256 hex-prefix buckets. Rationale: "a B+tree packs small records densely (no per-file block rounding) and range-scans them fast"; "the filesystem streams large values faster and without redb's ~2x page amplification." Three tables keyed `tree_id ++ item_id ++ content_digest`, so "**Byzantine-equivocating payloads (multiple blobs under one `CommitId`) coexist via the trailing digest**." Durability: external blobs are written **and fsynced before** the referencing transaction commits; worst case is a harmless orphan blob. [subduction_redb_storage/README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/subduction_redb_storage/README.md)
- **Known limitation, stated**: "**No blob GC.** `delete_*` removes the redb record but not the external blob file… this leaks only in proportion to document removals." Also: redb takes an **exclusive process lock**, so `migrate`/`inspect`/`purge` require the server stopped, and backup is "snapshot the whole root" with no incremental option. [subduction_redb_storage/README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/subduction_redb_storage/README.md)
- FS→redb migration is a first-class CLI verb: `subduction migrate --from … --to …`, one durable transaction per tree, **resumable**, **non-destructive**, `--dry-run`, copies `.keyhive/` state across, **one-way**. [subduction_redb_storage/MIGRATION.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/subduction_redb_storage/MIGRATION.md)
- **The LMDB adapter is not in this repo.** `packages/automerge-repo-storage-lmdb` is a **new package on `automerge-repo`'s `subductionjs` branch** (164 lines of `src/index.ts` + tests, MIT, added on that branch). If you were told "Subduction has an LMDB adapter," the truth is that the *automerge-repo consumer branch* gained one. [automerge-repo `subductionjs`: packages/automerge-repo-storage-lmdb](https://github.com/automerge/automerge-repo/tree/subductionjs/packages/automerge-repo-storage-lmdb)

## B6. What replaces what — Beelay → Subduction

- **Direct textual evidence is thin, and I will not overstate it.** The string "Beelay" appears **exactly once** in the Subduction repo: in `.github/workflows/dictionary.txt`, the spellcheck word list. There is no "successor to Beelay" statement in the repo. Say "no source found" for any in-repo succession claim. [subduction/.github/workflows/dictionary.txt](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/.github/workflows/dictionary.txt)
- **The lineage is nonetheless demonstrable by artifact:**
  - Subduction's README points its canonical Sedimentree design at `inkandswitch/keyhive/blob/main/design/sedimentree.md` — i.e. Subduction's core data structure *is* Beelay's, still hosted in Beelay's repo. [README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/README.md)
  - Keyhive's README lists `beelay-core` as "Auth-enabled sync over end-to-end encrypted data," pre-alpha. [keyhive/README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/keyhive/blob/main/README.md)
  - `automerge-repo` has a `beelay` branch last touched **2025-04-15** at version `2.0.0-alpha.23`, and a `subductionjs` branch last touched **2026-08-20** at `2.6.0-subduction.48`. The consumer-side integration effort moved from one to the other.
- **What Beelay did that Subduction now does**: Sedimentree-based sync of causal commit DAGs over ciphertext; the Keyhive-authorized sync relationship; the sync-server role. [keyhive/design/sedimentree.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/keyhive/blob/main/design/sedimentree.md)
- **What visibly changed**: Beelay was described as an **RPC protocol over a confidential transport** ("usable over any transport which can provide confidentiality — in practice HTTPS, WebSockets, or raw TLS"). Subduction is a **symmetric peer protocol** with its own Ed25519 mutual handshake, three pluggable transports (WebSocket / HTTP long-poll / Iroh-QUIC), a hand-rolled canonical binary codec replacing CBOR on the sync path, SipHash fingerprint reconciliation, per-peer monotonic counters, and an explicit relay-topology story. Its stated design goal set includes "**No Central Server**: True peer-to-peer synchronization." [README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/README.md), [design/handshake.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/handshake.md)
- **What was dropped, in-protocol and documented**: the explicit blob pull request/response (tags `0x02`/`0x03`, "retired"; blobs now ride inline with their commit/fragment). And a **rejected design**: the "want list" / pending-subscription activation flow — "Earlier designs considered a 'want list' where peers could express interest in documents they weren't yet authorized for… This was simplified: Keyhive handles auth notifications." [design/protocol.md §Message Tags](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/protocol.md), [design/sync/subscriptions.md §Why No Pending/Activation Flow?](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/sync/subscriptions.md)
- Also dropped without ceremony: `SedimentreeSummary` (full digests) "still exists for local diffing and testing but is **no longer used on the wire**." [design/sedimentree.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/sedimentree.md)

## B7. Adoption cost: `2.6.0-alpha.3` → `2.6.0-subduction.48`

Verified against npm and the `automerge-repo` git history.

- **These are two live release lines, not a version bump.** npm dist-tags for `@automerge/automerge-repo`: `latest → 2.6.0-alpha.3` (published **2026-08-07**), `subduction → 2.6.0-subduction.48` (published **2026-08-20**), plus `next → 2.6.0-alpha.2` and `authors → 2.7.0-authors.1`. The subduction line is a **parallel dist-tag off branch `subductionjs`**, 48 prereleases deep. Its `package.json` sets `"publishConfig": { "tag": "subduction" }`. [npm registry dist-tags](https://registry.npmjs.org/-/package/@automerge/automerge-repo/dist-tags), [automerge-repo `subductionjs` packages/automerge-repo/package.json](https://github.com/automerge/automerge-repo/blob/subductionjs/packages/automerge-repo/package.json)
- **Scale of the delta**: `git diff origin/main origin/subductionjs -- packages` = **139 files, +19,251 / −1,309**. Roughly a third of that is new test surface (a whole `test/subduction/` directory: Topology, WebSocket, WorkerWebSocket, ShutdownRace, AttachStorm, SaveResilience, BlobInterceptor…).
- **Is it drop-in? No.**
  - `@automerge/automerge-subduction` becomes a **hard dependency** of `@automerge/automerge-repo` (added to `dependencies`), along with `ws` and `isomorphic-ws`. You are taking on a Wasm module.
  - `Repo` gains a family of new constructor options: `subductionPolicy`, `subductionWebsocketEndpoints`, `subductionAdapters`, `subductionTimeouts`, `subductionCompactionDeleteConcurrency`, `subductionBlobInterceptor`, plus a `signer`. **A `SubductionSource` is constructed unconditionally** — with a `DummyStorageAdapter` when no storage is configured — so Subduction is wired into the Repo core, not bolted on.
  - New public surface: `initSubduction()`, `setupSubduction({ signer, storageAdapter })`, `SubductionStorageBridge`, `NetworkAdapterTransport` + `SUBDUCTION_MESSAGE_TYPE`, `WebSocketEndpoint` / `WorkerWebSocketEndpoint`, new Repo events `subduction-remote-heads` and `subduction-connection`.
  - Toolchain floor moves too: TypeScript `^6.0.3`, Vite `^8`, Vitest `4.1.7`, `uuid ^14`, plus Playwright for browser tests; the monorepo `catalog:` protocol is replaced with pinned ranges.
  - Substantial churn in files you may have subclassed or relied on: `Repo.ts` (+446/−…), `NetworkAdapter.ts`, `StorageAdapter*.ts`, `presence/*`, `synchronizer/*`, and `automerge-repo-storage-nodefs/src/index.ts` (+708).
  [package.json diff](https://github.com/automerge/automerge-repo/blob/subductionjs/packages/automerge-repo/package.json), [src/subduction/index.ts](https://github.com/automerge/automerge-repo/blob/subductionjs/packages/automerge-repo/src/subduction/index.ts), [src/initSubduction.ts](https://github.com/automerge/automerge-repo/blob/subductionjs/packages/automerge-repo/src/initSubduction.ts)
- **The good news — coexistence, not cutover.** Classic sync survives: `Repo.ts` distinguishes "classic-sync peers (keyed by an opaque storage UUID)" from "Subduction peers (keyed by their verifying-key peer id)," and `findClassic` remains (deprecated alias of `find`). Subduction messages can also tunnel over your **existing** automerge-repo `NetworkAdapter`s via `NetworkAdapterTransport` + `SUBDUCTION_MESSAGE_TYPE`, and over your **existing** `StorageAdapterInterface` via `SubductionStorageBridge`. The minimal opt-in really is three lines:
  ```ts
  const { subduction } = await setupSubduction({ signer: await WebCryptoSigner.setup(), storageAdapter })
  const repo = new Repo({ subduction })
  ```
  [src/Repo.ts](https://github.com/automerge/automerge-repo/blob/subductionjs/packages/automerge-repo/src/Repo.ts), [src/subduction/network.ts](https://github.com/automerge/automerge-repo/blob/subductionjs/packages/automerge-repo/src/subduction/network.ts)
- **Existing stored data: there is no migration.** The bridge writes an entirely new key space under prefix `"subduction"` (`subduction/ids/…`, `subduction/commits/…`, `subduction/blobs/…`, `subduction/fragments/…`, `subduction/fragment-blobs/…`), or `"subduction-interceptor"` when a blob interceptor is configured — deliberately separate "without separate prefixes, an interceptor-transformed commit and an untransformed commit with the same `CommitId` would collide at the same key and clobber each other." I grepped the branch: **no migration or importer for pre-existing automerge-repo snapshot/incremental chunks into sedimentree form.** Old data stays reachable through the classic path; it does not become Subduction-syncable by itself. The only ingest tool that exists is server-side and file-based: `automerge-subduction-ingest --server wss://… document.am`, which "reads an `.am` file, decomposes it into sedimentree fragments and loose commits, and uploads." That is your migration story today: export `.am`, re-ingest. [src/subduction/storage.ts](https://github.com/automerge/automerge-repo/blob/subductionjs/packages/automerge-repo/src/subduction/storage.ts), [automerge_subduction_ingest/README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/automerge_subduction_ingest/README.md)
- Maturity of the Rust side, for calibration: **~109k lines of Rust**, **583 `#[test]` + 460 `#[tokio::test]`**, bolero property tests, mutation testing (`.github/workflows/mutants.yml`), Playwright e2e, Criterion benches, Nix flake with NixOS + Home Manager modules, Prometheus/Grafana/Loki monitoring in `subduction_cli/monitoring/`. Far more built-out than onomancy — but still "**DO NOT use for production use cases at this time.**" [README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/README.md)

---

# C. Synthesis

## Where they overlap or compete

- **They do not compete. They stack, and the seam is clean.** Subduction moves bytes for a `SedimentreeId`; Onomancy answers *which document ID you meant* and *who may speak for it*. Onomancy explicitly declares replication out of scope: "replication and persistence belong to the substrate and the agent, never to these crates." [onomancy/README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/README.md)
- **They share one identity primitive** — the Automerge/Keyhive document ID as a 32-byte ed25519 verifying key — but use it for different jobs. Onomancy: `root_doc`, the thing a name denotes. Subduction: `SedimentreeId`, the thing a sync scopes to. Onomancy's *peer* identity concept doesn't exist; Subduction's `PeerId` is "verifying_key_bytes, 32 bytes, **no hashing**." [design/anchors.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/anchors.md), [subduction/design/protocol.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/protocol.md)
- **Shared house style, shared parts.** Both use bijou64 varints from `inkandswitch/bijou`, both use a 4-byte ASCII schema tag + version byte (`ONC\x00`/`ONR\x00`/`ONS\x00` vs `STC\x00`/`STF\x00`/`SUH\x00`/`SUM\x00`/`SUE\x00`), both use the `Signed<T> → Verified<T>` witness pattern, both hand-roll a canonical codec rather than trust CBOR. Onomancy's serialization spec names the convention as "**the schema convention shared with subduction**." Adopting one gets you most of the vocabulary for the other. [onomancy/specs/serialization.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/serialization.md)
- **The genuine overlap is `.well-known/` top-level key convention.** Onomancy claims `.well-known/onomancy/` in a document's own top-level map and asks upstream for agreement on the `.well-known/<owner>/<artifact>` prefix — "so an application's keys and a protocol's keys can share one map without either enumerating the other." If we adopt both, that namespace is a shared resource we must not collide with. [design/keyhive-coordination.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/keyhive-coordination.md)
- **Divergence worth watching**: both depend on Keyhive, but at *different pinned lineages*. Onomancy replays into `keyhive_core = "0.5"` behind a versioned `kh0` envelope; ARK/`@keyhive/keyhive` npm is a different lineage, and Onomancy's Ask 6 is literally "the ARK ↔ carriage encoding bridge… Certificates need PORTABLE proofs (static files); ARK is a live hive." Adopting both means we inherit that unresolved bridge. [design/keyhive-coordination.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/keyhive-coordination.md)
- **One real friction**: Onomancy's namestore is "a document's own top-level map" holding bare Automerge URLs as **scalar strings**. Subduction syncs opaque blobs and never reads document content. Fine — until you want a *relay* to resolve a name, which it structurally cannot. Resolution is always a plaintext-side operation. That is consistent with both designs, but it means naming is a capability of full members, never of infrastructure.

## Design ideas worth stealing even if we adopt neither

1. **Signed region vs. attached region.** "Signed = what the signer asserts (verifiable nowhere else); attached = what the world can check (self-authenticating; own lifecycle; swap freely)." Three consequences they enumerate: no freshness coupling (cold keys stay cold), keyless rotation repair, and no stale-evidence pinning. This maps directly onto our sealed-vs-attached tension in the crypto spine — and gives a principled test for which side of a signature any field belongs on. [design/certificate.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/certificate.md)
2. **`derive(store, now, decisions)` — state as pure derivation over a grow-only set.** "Every verdict is a deterministic function of what evidence you hold, never of when it arrived… arrival order is attacker-influenced (gossip)." Falls out: sync is set union; **no derived state ever crosses a boundary** (no "I saw X" marks on the wire, so asserted-conclusion poisoning is impossible by construction); ambiguity surfaces as *contested* rather than rewarding whoever gossiped first. This is the strongest single idea in either repo and it is exactly our "no hand-set weights one layer up" law, formalized. [specs/anchoring/binding-cache.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/anchoring/binding-cache.md)
3. **The generation-key chokepoint.** Revocation made verifier-visible by a *positive path-membership* check against one attested key, instead of a revocation list — because "negative facts can't travel in attacker-supplied records." Cost: one DNS touch per revocation, deliberately scheduled for the moment the operator is already rotating credentials. A general pattern for revocation without an oracle. [design/dns-binding.md §Generation Key](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/dns-binding.md)
4. **Graded verdicts instead of boolean validity, with events-vs-states discipline.** `fresh ✓ / stale ⚠ / invalid ✗` plus: staleness is a **badge, never a prompt**; interruptive UX is reserved for **events** (diffs in the derivation). Their `staleness-saturation` analysis — "a warning that is always on protects nothing," and every defense terminating in "surfaced to the user" draws on **one attention budget** — is the best treatment of this failure mode I have read. [design/security.md §Staleness Saturation](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/security.md)
5. **Pruning bounded by a derivation invariant.** "Pruning MUST NOT change any output of `derive`," with a decidable domination rule and a named residual (the far-future-serial record, capped by a **one-year deferral horizon**, explicitly the "one sanctioned exception"). Result: "Necessary storage grows with **ceremonies and introductions**… never with time, resolution count, or refresh churn." That is the right shape for our own zoning sweep. [specs/anchoring/binding-cache.md §Pruning](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/anchoring/binding-cache.md)
6. **Termination as a structural theorem, not a hop limit.** "Resolvers MUST NOT impose a hop limit as a substitute for the no-symlink invariant; the invariant is the termination proof." Plus the Lean trick: add a name-valued edge variant and the model *fails to compile*. Make the invariant break loudly rather than policing it at runtime. [specs/path-resolution.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/path-resolution.md), [design/verification.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/verification.md)
7. **Hash-derived, coordination-free partition boundaries** (Sedimentree). Leading-zero-byte depth + **reverse-DFS ordering for boundary stability** + **retained checkpoints so "supports" is decidable from metadata alone**. The reverse-DFS insight — "the parents of a commit never change" — is a general fix for any content-defined chunking over a DAG that concurrent writes can reorder. [keyhive/design/sedimentree.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/keyhive/blob/main/design/sedimentree.md)
8. **"Confirmation is just another sync."** No ack message type; run batch sync again and read the empty `requesting` set. "Don't add special cases, let the normal merge process handle it." [design/sync/batch.md §Confirmation](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/sync/batch.md)
9. **Authorize the author, not the sender.** Author identity comes from the signature; the sender is transport. Enables multi-hop relay with zero trust in relays, and makes revocation a silent forwarding-filter change. [design/security/rationale.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/security/rationale.md)
10. **A pre-registered rebuttal document.** `limitations.md` exists specifically so that when the project is tempted to claim "phishing-proof naming," the table is already written. We should copy this genre outright.
11. **Two-witness cheap-checks-first ordering**: decode → size → age → nonce probe (read-only) → *then* Ed25519 verify → *then* the only cache write. "The cheap checks run first so that demonstrably invalid wire bytes… are dropped before the receiver pays for an Ed25519 verify," and the read-only probe "can never put state in the cache that wasn't authenticated." [design/ephemeral.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/ephemeral.md)

## What contradicts a clockless / no-global-now / capability-first stance

**Onomancy — mostly compatible, three frictions:**

- ✅ Strongly aligned: "**Time is data.** No machine reads a clock… the only `now()` call in the workspace lives in the binary"; "Verification is clock-free; time only grades freshness"; "Clocks are used to grade chain freshness… No protocol step requires tight synchronization." A wrong clock yields wrong *grading*, "**never a forgery**." Rough clocks (hours, not months). [HACKING.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/HACKING.md), [design/assumptions.md §Time](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/assumptions.md)
- ⚠️ **Serial-as-timestamp is a wall-clock coupling with a security dependency.** Publishers MUST mint `n = max(now_ms, last + 1)`, and the security doc is explicit that the anti-poisoning bound "rests on a **publisher-side** rule… serials that do not track the clock cannot overtake a planted one on schedule, so **a shortcut in the minter silently weakens this defence**." A clockless publisher can still be monotone but cannot outbid a planted serial on schedule. [design/security.md §Ratchet Poisoning](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/security.md)
- ⚠️ The **5-minute deferral window** and the **one-year deferral horizon** are wall-clock constants baked into the derivation.
- ⚠️ **DNSSEC RRSIG windows are a global-now by construction.** Freshness is defined against absolute time you do not control. Onomancy's containment is that this is scoped to exactly one of three anchors — `~` and `automerge:` "owe DNS nothing" — which is precisely the right containment for us.
- ✅ **Capability-first is native**: authority is the Keyhive delegation graph; "revocation is just another edge in the delegation graph"; caches "confer no authority"; "no borrowed authority in your root doc"; "hints carry no authority." One misfit for us: **certificate issuance requires full admin** on the document, and hostname-scoped attenuation ("may bind `expede.wtf`, and nothing else") is an open upstream **Ask**, not a feature. Under our cap-tier model that is a coarse grant we would have to live with at v0. [design/keyhive-coordination.md §Ask 3](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/keyhive-coordination.md)

**Subduction — one hard contradiction, otherwise fine:**

- ❌ **The handshake requires an absolute wall clock.** `Challenge.timestamp` is Unix seconds, validated against the responder's `now` within `max_drift` (default 60s), with `Rejection::ClockDrift` as the failure. **A peer with no wall clock cannot connect.** Wall-clock time is a *liveness precondition*, not merely a grading input — which is the opposite of Onomancy's posture and the opposite of ours. [design/handshake.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/handshake.md)
- ❌ **The clock is treated as trusted-ish, not adversary-controlled.** `MAX_PLAUSIBLE_DRIFT` is a hard `const` of 10 minutes; the nonce cache's 12-minute window is sized against it; and their own assumption doc concedes that a backward time jump can "**allow replay of 'expired' nonces**." Our stance says wall-clock is adversary-controlled; theirs says it is bounded-adversarial. That gap is where a replay lives. [subduction_core/src/nonce_cache.rs](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/subduction_core/src/nonce_cache.rs), [design/assumptions.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/assumptions.md)
- ⚠️ Mitigating, and worth knowing: `now`, `max_drift`, and the nonce bucket duration are **all parameters**, not constants — core never calls a clock. A hardened deployment can widen the window; it pays in nonce-cache memory and replay window, and `MAX_PLAUSIBLE_DRIFT` still caps drift *correction*. Post-handshake, ordering is a **per-peer monotonic counter**, which is exactly the logical clock we want.
- ⚠️ The ephemeral layer adds a second wall-clock dependency (message-age checks, `JsClock` = `Date.now()`), but it is a separate crate we can decline.
- ✅ Capability-first is respected at the seam: authentication ⊥ authorization, `StoragePolicy`/`ConnectionPolicy` as pluggable traits, authorization against the **author**, revocation as a forwarding filter with no protocol message.
- ⚠️ **Not capability-shaped**: `subscriptions: Map<SedimentreeId, Set<PeerId>>` is a server-side roster, and relays **propagate subscriptions upstream to every other connected peer** by default. Under our attestation-seat ruling ("no ROSTER — a roster IS a global now"), a relay's subscription map plus the cleartext `IssuerVK` on every commit is a correlation surface we would need to reason about before running one. [design/sync/subscriptions.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/sync/subscriptions.md)

---

# THE FIVE THINGS THAT MATTER MOST TO US

**1. Subduction's handshake makes wall-clock time a liveness precondition — this is the one hard collision with our clockless lease model, and it is negotiable but not free.**
A peer with no wall clock cannot complete a Subduction handshake; one whose clock is off by more than `max_drift` gets `ClockDrift`. Their own assumptions doc admits a backward time jump can "allow replay of 'expired' nonces." The escape hatch is real — `now`, `max_drift`, and `NonceCache` bucket duration are all *parameters*; `subduction_core` never reads a clock — so we can widen the window and pay in nonce-cache memory. But `MAX_PLAUSIBLE_DRIFT = 10 min` is a hard `const`, and the documented 12-minute nonce window is sized against it, so widening naively reopens replay silently. Decide this *before* adoption, not after. Everything post-handshake is a clean per-peer monotonic counter and needs no clock at all. [handshake.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/handshake.md) · [nonce_cache.rs](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/subduction_core/src/nonce_cache.rs) · [assumptions.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/assumptions.md)

**2. `derive(store, now, decisions)` — verdicts as a pure derivation over a grow-only set, with no derived state ever on the wire.**
This is the single most transferable idea in either repo, and it is our own "nothing gets pre-assigned" law given a normative, 8-stage, acyclic formulation. Sync becomes set union; arrival order is structurally not an input; ambiguity surfaces as *contested* instead of rewarding the fastest gossiper; and because no ratchet counter or "I saw X" mark crosses a boundary, asserted-conclusion poisoning is **impossible by construction** rather than filtered. It pairs with a pruning rule bounded by its own invariant, so storage grows with *ceremonies and introductions*, never with time or traffic. Steal this whether or not we adopt a line of their code. [binding-cache.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/specs/anchoring/binding-cache.md)

**3. Signed region vs. attached region — the discipline that keeps cold keys cold.**
"Signed = what the signer asserts; attached = what the world can check." Because the DNSSEC chain, the delegation chain, and the lineage all sit *outside* the signature, freshness refresh and post-rotation chain repair are **keyless** — anyone can courier fresher evidence, which is what makes staleness "a community good" instead of an owner obligation. The three named failure modes of signing them (freshness coupling, rotation repair, stale-evidence pinning) give us a decision procedure for which side of a signature any field belongs on — directly applicable to our @cad seal vs. cas-stage split. And note the deliberate exception: `predecessor` is *inside* the signature because it is a claim an attacker must not strip. [certificate.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/certificate.md)

**4. Adoption of Subduction via automerge-repo is a fork-line commitment, not a version bump — and there is no data migration.**
`2.6.0-subduction.48` is a *separate npm dist-tag* off branch `subductionjs` (48 prereleases; +19,251/−1,309 across 139 files vs `main`), while `latest` remains `2.6.0-alpha.3`. It makes the `@automerge/automerge-subduction` Wasm module a hard dependency of `automerge-repo`, constructs a `SubductionSource` unconditionally inside `Repo`, and moves the toolchain floor (TS 6, Vite 8, Vitest 4). The good news: classic sync coexists, and our existing `NetworkAdapter`/`StorageAdapter` implementations are reusable via `NetworkAdapterTransport` and `SubductionStorageBridge`. The bad news: the bridge writes an entirely new `subduction/…` key space, and **no importer exists** for pre-existing automerge-repo storage — the only path is export `.am` → `automerge-subduction-ingest`. Price that migration before committing. [npm dist-tags](https://registry.npmjs.org/-/package/@automerge/automerge-repo/dist-tags) · [subductionjs Repo.ts](https://github.com/automerge/automerge-repo/blob/subductionjs/packages/automerge-repo/src/Repo.ts) · [subduction/storage.ts](https://github.com/automerge/automerge-repo/blob/subductionjs/packages/automerge-repo/src/subduction/storage.ts)

**5. "Encryption-friendly" does not mean metadata-quiet — and Onomancy is a specification with a half-built instrument.**
Subduction relays learn the `SedimentreeId`, the full causal DAG shape, per-commit blob sizes, subscription sets, timing, **and the author's raw Ed25519 verifying key in the clear on every commit** — an authorship graph, not just a size channel. Their entire stated mitigation is "consider padding." Combined with a server-side `Map<SedimentreeId, Set<PeerId>>` subscription roster that relays propagate upstream by default, this is a correlation surface to weigh against our face-plane law before we run a relay. On the other side: Onomancy's normative specs are extraordinary — the binding-cache derivation, the pending/contested doctrine, tenure grading — but the reference implementation **does not write the decision document at all**, so acceptance-on-use never fires, incumbency is never recorded, and the Lean model is a directory that does not yet exist. Read Onomancy as a *design we adopt*, not a library we depend on; read Subduction as a *library we might depend on* whose threat model we must extend ourselves. [threats.md T8](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/security/threats.md) · [protocol.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/subduction/blob/main/design/protocol.md) · [onomancer/README.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/onomancer/README.md) · [verification.md](https://github.com/inkandswitch/onomancy/blob/main/design/verification.md)
````

